1x1 controller 15 (MCU-ready STM32 HAL-style implementation)

below is a complete, MCU-ready STM32 HAL-style implementation that moves the adaptive scoring and morphing on-board so the microcontroller can make autonomous DE5 decisions without relying on a PC.
What you get:
    • main.c — HAL init, main loop, safety watchdog, PWM/ADC placeholders, and periodic control tick.
    • de5_control.h / de5_control.c — DE5 table structure, scoring, normalization, morphing logic, weight management, rate-limits, and safety clamps.
    • uart_cmd.h / uart_cmd.c — UART command parser to manage rows, weights, start/stop, and telemetry toggles; simple text protocol compatible with the PC GUI.
    • Comments and README-style notes inside code explaining how to adapt PWM/ADC pins, how to change the DE5 parameter table, and what the protocol lines look like.
    • Telemetry streaming (CSV-like) to the serial port for logging/visualization.
    • Safety limits: morph cooldown, max row step per morph, anti-flapping hysteresis, emergency stop, optional current-limit check.
This is a structured, production-minded skeleton you can drop into an STM32CubeIDE project (connect HAL init from CubeMX). It purposely leaves hardware-specific details (pin assignments, exact timer/ADC channels) obvious and marked with TODOs so you can plug your CubeMX-generated handles.

Files (all provided below)
    1. de5_control.h
    2. de5_control.c
    3. uart_cmd.h
    4. uart_cmd.c
    5. main.c
Put them into your STM32CubeIDE project, include the CubeMX-generated stm32xxx_hal.h and main.h as usual, and wire the timers/ADCs/UART handles referenced in the TODOs.

Protocol (UART)
ASCII commands (line terminated with \r or \n):
    • R<n> — set active DE5 row to <n> (example R5)
    • START — enable on-board adaptive morphing and control loop
    • STOP — disable adaptive morphing (safe state)
    • W T <0.0..1.0> — set torque weight (example W T 0.5)
    • W E <0.0..1.0> — set efficiency weight
    • W P <0.0..1.0> — set temperature weight (P = "thermal")
    • W N <n> — set morph max step (integer)
    • GET — prints state summary
    • STREAM ON / STREAM OFF — enable/disable telemetry streaming
    • SAV — (placeholder) save current weights to persistent storage (implementation optional)
Telemetry stream format (CSV):
<t_ms>,<I_A>,<Vcmd>,<I_tgt>,<T_C>,<torque>,<eff>\r\n
Example: 1623456,54.2,0.12,60.0,46.3,8271.06,0.9942

Notes before you paste & compile
    • Replace htimX, hadcX, huartX with your CubeMX handles (e.g., htim1, hadc1, huart2).
    • Implement read_adc_current() and read_temperature_C() functions per your sensor hardware; simple stubs are provided.
    • DE5_TABLE is stored in flash as a const array. Edit values to match your DE5 CSV. For dynamic updates you can implement a simple flash-write routine or use an external EEPROM.
    • PWM outputs are set via set_pwm_duty_coil_X(duty) stubs — map them to timers in CubeMX.
    • The code uses integer-friendly math and a few floating ops (float). Use -mfpu/-mfloat-abi in project settings if using Cortex-M4F/M7.

de5_control.h
/* de5_control.h
 * Onboard DE5 adaptive scoring + morphing (STM32 HAL-style)
 *
 * Public API:
 *   de5_init();
 *   de5_tick(dt_ms);
 *   de5_set_weights(w_torque, w_eff, w_temp);
 *   de5_set_row(n);
 *   de5_start(); de5_stop();
 *   de5_handle_emergency_stop();
 *   de5_stream_telemetry(onoff);
 *
 * NOTE: Replace TODO hardware functions with your CubeMX handles.
 */

#ifndef DE5_CONTROL_H
#define DE5_CONTROL_H

#include <stdint.h>
#include <stdbool.h>

#define MAX_DE5_ROWS 64
#define TELEMETRY_BUF_LEN 128

typedef struct {
    uint16_t row_id;
    float torque;      // reference torque metric
    float efficiency;  // reference efficiency metric
    float est_temp;    // estimated temperature proxy
} DE5Row_t;

typedef struct {
    bool running;
    uint16_t active_row;
    float w_torque;
    float w_eff;
    float w_temp;
    uint32_t morph_cooldown_ms;
    uint8_t morph_step_max;
    bool stream_telemetry;
} DE5State_t;

/* Initialize DE5 module and load table (table stored in flash) */
void de5_init(void);

/* Call from main loop at regular interval (dt_ms is delta time) */
void de5_tick(uint32_t dt_ms);

/* Set weights (no persistence) */
void de5_set_weights(float w_torque, float w_eff, float w_temp);

/* Set morph parameters */
void de5_set_morph_params(uint32_t cooldown_ms, uint8_t step_max);

/* Manually set active row (immediate) */
void de5_set_row(uint16_t row);

/* Start/stop autonomous morphing */
void de5_start(void);
void de5_stop(void);

/* Emergency stop (disables outputs immediately) */
void de5_handle_emergency_stop(void);

/* telemetry streaming on/off */
void de5_stream_telemetry(bool on);

/* State getter */
DE5State_t de5_get_state(void);

/* Hardware hooks to implement in your project (weak or real) */
float hw_read_current_A(void);      // read coil/phase current
float hw_read_temperature_C(void);  // read coil temp
void hw_set_pwm_duty(float duty_left, float duty_right); // set output PWM [-1..1] normalized
uint32_t hw_get_millis(void);       // millisecond uptime
void hw_safe_shutdown_outputs(void); // set PWMs to safe / zero

#endif /* DE5_CONTROL_H */

